and flipped vertically, and the bottom band repeats the upper band. This exact orientation is inferred from one demonstration only. A uniform 2×3 input was correctly transformed to a uniform 6×6 grid, but uniform inputs cannot distinguish tiling orientations.","path":"general","scope":"global","reference":{},"arguments":{},"metadata":{"scope":"global"},"source":"refine","created_at":"2026-09-29T13:00:43.541Z","updated_at":"2026-09-29T13:00:43.541Z","version":1},"after":{"id":"continual_arc_task_a2bb13dc_reflected_tiling","kind":"memory","title":"Continual-ARC task-a2bb13dc: expand by reflected tiling (general, v2)","content":"Global task-specific memory for Continual-ARC task-a2bb13dc. The demonstrated 8×6 input expands to a 24×12 output (3H×2W) using the previously recorded reflected-copy arrangement. Preserve that arrangement as provisional: its precise orientation is supported by only one nonuniform demonstration. An additional accepted test used the uniform 2×1 input [[2],[2]] and produced a 6×2 output filled with 2s. This confirms the 3H×2W dimensions but cannot distinguish or provide evidence for any tile orientations. Do not treat the uniform test as orientation evidence.","path":"general","scope":"global","reference":{},"arguments":{},"metadata":{"scope":"global"},"source":"refine","created_at":"2026-09-29T13:00:43.541Z","updated_at":"2026-09-29T13:05:44.515Z","version":2},"applied":true}],"harnessStatePath":"/home/ubuntu/continual-arc-baselines/.workbench/runs/adhoc-primeagentlearner-1790682293/attempts/52117ae0ff214dc5897fae72cc93757c/system/state/agent/harness/harness_state.json","scope":"global"}
{"id":"refine_20260929130714748","summary":"Create a global memory for Continual-ARC task-5d804742 describing how to complete missing cells in periodic patterns.","rationale":"Three demonstrations and the correctly solved test show that zero-valued cells can mark omissions in periodic patterns. The examples include both horizontally uniform stripes with a vertical period and a diagonally periodic 2D pattern, so the rule should infer the pattern's orientation and period from each input rather than assume fixed dimensions or period.","expectedOutcome":"On recurrence, infer the smallest periodic tiling consistent with the visible nonzero cells, fill omissions while preserving known values, and check the completed grid against the observed constraints.","appliedEdits":[{"action":"create","kind":"memory","id":"continual_arc_task_5d804742_complete_missing_cells_in_periodic_pattern","title":"Continual-ARC task-5d804742: complete missing cells in a periodic pattern","content":"Global verified rule for Continual-ARC task-5d804742, supported by three demonstrations and a correctly solved test. Zero-valued cells or rows are omissions in a periodic pattern. Infer the pattern's orientation and smallest period compatible with all visible nonzero cells; complete the grid by periodic continuation while preserving known values. Do not assume a fixed period, orientation, or dimensions. Demonstrations included horizontally uniform stripes with vertical periods 8 and 4, and a diagonally periodic 2D pattern. In the solved 10×10 test, the minimum consistent vertical period was 6, yielding row colors [7,6,9,1,4,6,7,6,9,1]. Validate that the inferred period agrees with every known cell before filling.","metadata":{"scope":"global"},"reason":"The examples and accepted test establish a reusable periodic-pattern completion rule across more than one orientation.","after":{"id":"continual_arc_task_5d804742_complete_missing_cells_in_periodic_pattern","kind":"memory","title":"Continual-ARC task-5