{"id":"refine_20260929131958816","summary":"Updated the global task-5d804742 memory with a second verified periodic-pattern recurrence.","rationale":"The supplied trajectory includes a demo and an accepted test solution that strengthen the existing task memory. The test’s smallest consistent rectangular tile is 3×3, and checking for conflicting colors at matching tile residues avoids unsupported period guesses.","expectedOutcome":"On recurrence, infer a compact rectangular period from known nonzero cells, treat zeros as omissions, and repeat the consistent tile across the grid; validate by checking that no differently colored observations share a tile residue.","appliedEdits":[{"action":"update","kind":"memory","id":"continual_arc_task_5d804742_complete_missing_cells_in_periodic_pattern","title":"Continual-ARC task-5d804742: complete missing cells in periodic patterns","content":"Global verified rule for Continual-ARC task-5d804742. Zeros are omissions in a periodic color pattern. Infer a rectangular period consistent with the visible nonzero cells, then repeat the inferred tile across the grid while preserving all known values. Compare candidate periods by checking whether any observations map to the same tile residue with different colors; prefer the smallest consistent tile, but avoid assigning unseen tile cells without sufficient evidence. Evidence includes an 8×8 demo with uniform rows 9, 0, 8, 3, 7, 6, 0, 1, whose output completes the six-row sequence 9, 1, 8, 3, 7, 6 and repeats it. In a correctly solved 15×15 test, color 1 appeared on anti-diagonal r+c=5, color 2 on r+c=22, and color 4 at r+c=27. The smallest consistent rectangular tile was 3×3; the completed pattern followed (r+c) mod 3 = 0 → 4, 1 → 2, 2 → 1. Validate the candidate tile against every known nonzero cell before filling the grid.","metadata":{"scope":"global"},"reason":"Adds the accepted second recurrence and concrete period-selection and validation evidence to the existing task memory.","befo