{"id":"refine_20260929135949854","summary":"Updated the existing global task-5d804742 memory with the verified diagonal-periodicity rule.","rationale":"The harness already contains a global memory for this task; the successful test and four demonstrations support adding this rule there rather than creating a duplicate.","expectedOutcome":"On recurrence, recognize clue colors on constant column-minus-row diagonals, infer the smallest consistent period, and fill the grid by diagonal residue; validate that all clue diagonals retain their colors.","appliedEdits":[{"action":"update","kind":"memory","id":"continual_arc_task_5d804742_complete_missing_cells_in_periodic_pattern","title":"Continual-ARC task-5d804742: complete periodic patterns, including diagonal coloring","content":"Global verified rule for Continual-ARC task-5d804742. Zeros or otherwise missing cells can be omissions in a periodic color pattern; infer a compact period consistent with visible clues and fill while preserving known values. In the verified diagonal variant, clue colors lie on down-right diagonals of constant d = column - row. Infer the smallest period consistent with clue offsets and colors, assign colors by d modulo that period, and fill every cell accordingly. In the solved 8×8 test, clues at d=-5,-4,-1,3,7 had colors 1,3,9,6,5 respectively, yielding period 5 with residues 0→1, 1→3, 2→5, 3→6, 4→9. Check that all clue diagonals agree with the inferred residue mapping.","metadata":{"scope":"global","evidence":"Four demonstrations and a correctly solved test"},"reason":"Preserve the successful diagonal-periodicity inference for future recurrences of this task while extending the existing memory instead of duplicating it.","before":{"id":"continual_arc_task_5d804742_complete_missing_cells_in_periodic_pattern","kind":"memory","title":"Continual-ARC task-5d804742: complete missing cells in periodic patterns","content":"Global verified rule for Continual-ARC task-5d804742. Zeros are omissions in a periodic color pattern. Infer a rectangular period consistent with the visible nonzero cells, then repeat the inferred tile across the grid while preserving all known values. Compare candidate periods by checking whether any observations map to the same tile residue with different colors; prefer the smallest consistent tile, but avoid assigning unseen tile cells without sufficient evidence. Evidence includes an 8×8 demo with uniform rows 9, 0, 8, 3, 7, 6, 0, 1, whose output completes the six-row sequence 9, 1, 8, 3, 7, 6 and repeats it. In a correctly solved 15×15 test, color 1 appeared on anti-diagonal r+c=5, color 2 on r+c=22, and color 4 at r+c=27. The smallest consistent rectangular tile was 3×3; the completed pattern followed (r+c) mod 3 = 0 → 4, 1 → 2, 2 → 1. Validate the candidate tile against every known nonzero cell before filling the grid.","path":"general","scope":"global","reference":{},"arguments":{},"metadata":{"scope":"global"},"source":"refine","created_at":"2026-09-29T13:07:23.189Z","updated_at":"2026-09-29T13:20:05.534Z","version":2},"after":{"id":"continual_arc_task_5d804742_complete_missing_cells_in_periodic_pattern","kind":"memory","title":"Continual-ARC task-5d804742: complete periodic patterns, including diagonal coloring","content":"Global verified rule for Continual-ARC task-5d804742. Zeros or otherwise missing cells can be omissions in a periodic color pattern; infer a compact period consistent with visible clues and fill while preserving known values. In the verified diagonal variant, clue colors lie on down-right diagonals of constant d = column - row. Infer the smallest period consistent with clue offsets and colors, assign colors by d modulo that period, and fill every cell accordingly. In the solved 8×8 test, clues at d=-5,-4,-1,3,7 had colors 1,3,9,6,5 respectively, yielding period 5 with residues 0→1, 1→3, 2→5, 3→6, 4→9. Check that all clue diagonals agree with the inferred residue mapping.","path":"general","scope":"global","referenc